const passages = /* #__PURE__ */ JSON.parse("[{\"passageNumber\":1,\"title\":\"Underwater Archaeology: Uncovering Submerged Civilizations\",\"subtitle\":\"How advances in remote sensing and underwater robotics are revolutionizing marine archaeology\",\"wordCount\":820,\"text\":\"Paragraph A\\nFor centuries, the exploration of submerged human heritage was confined to shallow coastal waters accessible only to sponge divers and free-diving salvage crews. The vast expanses of the world's continental shelves—many of which were dry land during the Last Glacial Maximum when global sea levels were roughly 120 meters lower than today—remained shrouded in maritime obscurity. However, during the late twentieth and early twenty-first centuries, the convergence of saturation diving, side-scan sonar, multibeam bathymetry, and autonomous underwater vehicles (AUVs) transformed underwater archaeology into an exacting scientific discipline. Today, researchers can map and excavate submerged prehistoric settlements and shipwreck sites at depths exceeding three thousand meters with millimetric precision.\\n\\nParagraph B\\nA key driver of this revolution is marine acoustic imaging. Traditional optical cameras suffer severe limitations in the marine environment due to rapid light attenuation and turbidity caused by suspended sediments. Acoustic sensors, by contrast, transmit sonic pulses through the water column and measure the time and intensity of the returning echoes. Side-scan sonar generates photorealistic acoustic representations of the seabed topography, revealing the distinct geometric silhouettes of ancient wooden hulls, ballast stone mounds, and submerged stone foundations. When coupled with sub-bottom profilers, which emit lower-frequency acoustic waves capable of penetrating several meters into soft sea-floor sediments, archaeologists can detect buried wooden timbers and prehistoric flint tools hidden completely beneath marine silt.\\n\\nParagraph C\\nThe advantages of marine environments over terrestrial archaeological contexts are profound. Terrestrial archaeological sites are subject to severe weathering, agricultural plowing, bacterial decomposition, and centuries of human recycling and looting. In contrast, deep cold water, particularly in anoxic marine basins such as the Black Sea, lacks dissolved oxygen and wood-boring organisms such as the shipworm (Teredo navalis). Under these uniquely sterile conditions, organic materials that would disintegrate on land within decades—including ship masts, rope rigging, carved wooden figureheads, woven textiles, and even leather sandals—remain preserved in pristine condition for millennia. In 2018, an Anglo-Bulgarian expedition identified an intact ancient Greek commercial vessel dating to 400 BCE at a depth of two kilometers in the Black Sea, complete with its rudder and rowing benches undisturbed.\\n\\nParagraph D\\nDespite these scientific successes, deep-sea archaeology confronts formidable ethical and logistical dilemmas. Deep-water expeditions require oceanographic research vessels costing upwards of fifty thousand dollars per day, creating immense financial barriers for academic institutions. Consequently, private commercial salvage firms have historically dominated deep-sea shipwreck recovery, treating historical wreck sites as commercial asset-mining opportunities to harvest precious gold bullion, silver coins, and valuable porcelain for private auction. To counter commercial exploitation, UNESCO ratified the 2001 Convention on the Protection of the Underwater Cultural Heritage, which establishes an international legal framework prohibiting the commercial trafficking of historic submerged artifacts and declaring in situ preservation—leaving cultural artifacts undisturbed on the seafloor—as the preferred preservation protocol.\\n\\nParagraph E\\nLooking toward the future, the integration of photogrammetry and robotic manipulators is dramatically minimizing the need for physical artifact retrieval. Autonomous underwater vehicles equipped with stereo-cameras take tens of thousands of high-resolution overlapping photographs of a submerged ruin. Sophisticated photogrammetric software stitches these images into millimeter-accurate three-dimensional digital replicas. Archaeologists and the public can explore these virtual underwater museums via virtual reality headsets without disturbing a single grain of sediment or exposing delicate organic relics to the degrading atmosphere above.\",\"questionGroups\":[{\"groupInstruction\":\"Do the following statements agree with the information given in Reading Passage 1? In boxes 1–7 on your answer sheet, write:\\nTRUE if the statement agrees with the information\\nFALSE if the statement contradicts the information\\nNOT GIVEN if there is no information on this\",\"questionType\":\"true_false_not_given\",\"questions\":[{\"id\":\"a3-q1\",\"questionNumber\":1,\"prompt\":\"During the Last Glacial Maximum, sea levels worldwide were over one hundred meters lower than current levels.\",\"correctAnswer\":\"TRUE\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph A explicitly states that during the Last Glacial Maximum 'global sea levels were roughly 120 meters lower than today'.\",\"evidence\":\"many of which were dry land during the Last Glacial Maximum when global sea levels were roughly 120 meters lower than today\"},{\"id\":\"a3-q2\",\"questionNumber\":2,\"prompt\":\"Optical cameras are more effective than acoustic sonar in murky or sediment-rich waters.\",\"correctAnswer\":\"FALSE\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph B states optical cameras suffer limitations due to turbidity, whereas acoustic sensors transmit sound through the water column unaffected by light loss.\",\"evidence\":\"Traditional optical cameras suffer severe limitations in the marine environment due to rapid light attenuation and turbidity caused by suspended sediments. Acoustic sensors, by contrast, transmit sonic pulses through the water column\"},{\"id\":\"a3-q3\",\"questionNumber\":3,\"prompt\":\"Sub-bottom profilers emit higher-frequency sonic waves than side-scan sonar devices.\",\"correctAnswer\":\"FALSE\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph B explains that sub-bottom profilers emit 'lower-frequency acoustic waves capable of penetrating several meters into soft sea-floor sediments'.\",\"evidence\":\"When coupled with sub-bottom profilers, which emit lower-frequency acoustic waves capable of penetrating several meters into soft sea-floor sediments\"},{\"id\":\"a3-q4\",\"questionNumber\":4,\"prompt\":\"The shipworm Teredo navalis cannot survive in waters completely lacking dissolved oxygen.\",\"correctAnswer\":\"TRUE\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph C notes that anoxic marine basins like the Black Sea lack dissolved oxygen and therefore lack wood-boring organisms like the shipworm (Teredo navalis).\",\"evidence\":\"particularly in anoxic marine basins such as the Black Sea, lacks dissolved oxygen and wood-boring organisms such as the shipworm (Teredo navalis).\"},{\"id\":\"a3-q5\",\"questionNumber\":5,\"prompt\":\"The Greek vessel discovered in the Black Sea in 2018 was damaged by heavy commercial fishing trawlers.\",\"correctAnswer\":\"NOT GIVEN\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph C mentions the discovery of the intact Greek vessel with its rudder and rowing benches undisturbed, but does not state anything about damage from commercial fishing trawlers.\",\"evidence\":\"In 2018, an Anglo-Bulgarian expedition identified an intact ancient Greek commercial vessel dating to 400 BCE at a depth of two kilometers in the Black Sea, complete with its rudder and rowing benches undisturbed.\"},{\"id\":\"a3-q6\",\"questionNumber\":6,\"prompt\":\"UNESCO's 2001 Convention prioritizes leaving submerged cultural heritage in its original underwater location.\",\"correctAnswer\":\"TRUE\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph D states that the UNESCO convention declares 'in situ preservation—leaving cultural artifacts undisturbed on the seafloor—as the preferred preservation protocol.'\",\"evidence\":\"declaring in situ preservation—leaving cultural artifacts undisturbed on the seafloor—as the preferred preservation protocol.\"},{\"id\":\"a3-q7\",\"questionNumber\":7,\"prompt\":\"Virtual reality models of underwater wreck sites have entirely replaced physical academic diving expeditions.\",\"correctAnswer\":\"NOT GIVEN\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph E explains that photogrammetry allows virtual exploration, but never claims that physical diving expeditions have been completely replaced.\",\"evidence\":\"Archaeologists and the public can explore these virtual underwater museums via virtual reality headsets without disturbing a single grain of sediment\"}]},{\"groupInstruction\":\"Complete the summary below. Choose NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS from the passage for each answer. Write your answers in boxes 8–13 on your answer sheet.\",\"questionType\":\"summary_completion\",\"questions\":[{\"id\":\"a3-q8\",\"questionNumber\":8,\"prompt\":\"Autonomous underwater vehicles allow modern researchers to conduct marine excavations with ____ accuracy.\",\"correctAnswer\":\"millimetric\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph A states researchers can map and excavate sites 'with millimetric precision'.\",\"evidence\":\"Today, researchers can map and excavate submerged prehistoric settlements and shipwreck sites at depths exceeding three thousand meters with millimetric precision.\"},{\"id\":\"a3-q9\",\"questionNumber\":9,\"prompt\":\"Unlike standard cameras, acoustic sensors are not hindered by water turbidity or ____.\",\"correctAnswer\":\"light attenuation\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph B explains that optical cameras suffer from 'rapid light attenuation and turbidity'.\",\"evidence\":\"Traditional optical cameras suffer severe limitations in the marine environment due to rapid light attenuation and turbidity caused by suspended sediments.\"},{\"id\":\"a3-q10\",\"questionNumber\":10,\"prompt\":\"Deep ocean waters that are anoxic lack the ____ needed for wood-eating organisms to thrive.\",\"correctAnswer\":\"dissolved oxygen\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph C states that anoxic marine basins lack 'dissolved oxygen and wood-boring organisms'.\",\"evidence\":\"particularly in anoxic marine basins such as the Black Sea, lacks dissolved oxygen and wood-boring organisms such as the shipworm\"},{\"id\":\"a3-q11\",\"questionNumber\":11,\"prompt\":\"Historic wrecks have frequently been exploited by private commercial salvage firms searching for precious metals and ____.\",\"correctAnswer\":\"porcelain\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph D mentions private firms harvesting 'precious gold bullion, silver coins, and valuable porcelain for private auction'.\",\"evidence\":\"harvest precious gold bullion, silver coins, and valuable porcelain for private auction.\"},{\"id\":\"a3-q12\",\"questionNumber\":12,\"prompt\":\"The principle of ____ entails protecting submerged historical items by keeping them directly on the ocean floor.\",\"correctAnswer\":\"in situ preservation\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph D refers to 'in situ preservation—leaving cultural artifacts undisturbed on the seafloor'.\",\"evidence\":\"declaring in situ preservation—leaving cultural artifacts undisturbed on the seafloor—as the preferred preservation protocol.\"},{\"id\":\"a3-q13\",\"questionNumber\":13,\"prompt\":\"Software uses high-resolution overlapping images to construct detailed three-dimensional ____ of ancient wreck sites.\",\"correctAnswer\":\"digital replicas\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph E describes stitching images into 'millimeter-accurate three-dimensional digital replicas'.\",\"evidence\":\"Sophisticated photogrammetric software stitches these images into millimeter-accurate three-dimensional digital replicas.\"}]}]},{\"passageNumber\":2,\"title\":\"The Neuroscience of Sleep and Memory Consolidation\",\"subtitle\":\"Investigating the synaptic mechanisms that transform daily experiences into durable memories\",\"wordCount\":840,\"text\":\"Paragraph A\\nFor centuries, sleep was conceptualized as a passive biological state—a temporary shutdown of consciousness during which the brain resting from the rigors of wakefulness remains largely dormant. Over the last four decades, however, electroencephalography (EEG) and functional neuroimaging have radically overturned this reductionist view. Sleep is now recognized as a highly dynamic, metabolically demanding neurochemical process during which the brain undergoes extensive synaptic reorganization. Far from being inactive, the sleeping brain actively selectively reviews, refines, and reorganizes cognitive representations acquired during waking hours, transforming fragile short-term impressions into robust, long-term semantic knowledge.\\n\\nParagraph B\\nTo comprehend memory consolidation, one must understand the two-stage memory architecture of the human brain. During wakefulness, new episodic experiences—such as memorizing a list of vocabulary words or navigating an unfamiliar city—are initially encoded within the hippocampus. The hippocampus serves as a fast-learning, temporary storage buffer with rapid neuroplasticity but limited capacity. Conversely, the neocortex possesses vast long-term storage capacity but learns slowly, requiring repeated, distributed reactivation to integrate new concepts without overwriting existing neural associations. Without a mechanism to transfer information from the temporary hippocampal repository to the permanent neocortical networks, previously acquired memories would be catastrophically overwritten by subsequent waking experiences.\\n\\nParagraph C\\nThis vital dialogue between the hippocampus and neocortex takes place primarily during non-rapid eye movement (NREM) slow-wave sleep. During deep NREM sleep, the brain generates slow electrical oscillations that sweep across the neocortex at a frequency of approximately one cycle per second. These slow waves coordinate sharp-wave ripples—high-frequency bursts of neuronal firing originating within the CA3 region of the hippocampus. During these ripples, the exact neural firing sequences that encoded waking experiences are 'replayed' in fast-forward mode, up to twenty times faster than real-time. This accelerated replay repeatedly broadcasts new memory traces to the receptive neocortex, gradually etching them into long-term synaptic connections.\\n\\nParagraph D\\nMeanwhile, sleep spindles—brief, rhythmic bursts of 11 to 16 Hertz brain activity generated by the thalamus—act as gatekeepers during this information transfer. Spindle density increases sharply following intensive learning sessions, and the precise temporal coupling between slow waves and sleep spindles predicts the degree of next-day memory recall. Crucially, sleep spindles create localized influxes of calcium ions into cortical dendrites, triggering the intracellular signaling cascades necessary for long-term potentiation—the physical strengthening of synapses that forms the biological basis of memory retention.\\n\\nParagraph E\\nWhile NREM sleep consolidates declarative and factual information, rapid eye movement (REM) sleep performs a fundamentally different cognitive operation. Characterized by rapid desynchronized brainwaves and vivid dreams, REM sleep is associated with emotional recalibration and creative problem-solving. During REM sleep, the brain's levels of noradrenaline—a key chemical associated with anxiety and emotional stress—drop to zero. This neurochemically unique, stress-free milieu allows the brain to process traumatic or emotionally charged episodic memories, stripping away the painful visceral charge while retaining the informational essence of the experience. Furthermore, REM sleep promotes associative cross-linking, allowing the brain to forge novel connections between seemingly unrelated concepts, explaining why breakthroughs in mathematical and artistic dilemmas frequently occur upon awakening.\\n\\nParagraph F\\nThe practical implications of these discoveries for educational pedagogy and public health are profound. Chronic sleep restriction, endemic among university students and modern corporate workers, truncates slow-wave sleep and REM cycles. Deprived of adequate sleep, individuals experience acute deficits in both memory encoding and consolidation. When students pull 'all-nighter' study sessions before examinations, the hippocampal temporary buffer becomes saturated and unstable; information fails to transition to the neocortex and is rapidly forgotten within forty-eight hours. Neurologists advocate that prioritizing sleep should be regarded not as a dispensable luxury, but as an indispensable component of cognitive competence.\",\"questionGroups\":[{\"groupInstruction\":\"Reading Passage 2 has six paragraphs, A–F. Which paragraph contains the following information? Write the correct letter, A–F, in boxes 14–19 on your answer sheet.\",\"questionType\":\"matching_information\",\"questions\":[{\"id\":\"a3-q14\",\"questionNumber\":14,\"prompt\":\"an explanation of how the brain removes negative emotional distress from past memories\",\"correctAnswer\":\"E\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph E describes how during REM sleep the absence of noradrenaline allows the brain to process traumatic memories, 'stripping away the painful visceral charge while retaining the informational essence'.\",\"evidence\":\"This neurochemically unique, stress-free milieu allows the brain to process traumatic or emotionally charged episodic memories, stripping away the painful visceral charge while retaining the informational essence\"},{\"id\":\"a3-q15\",\"questionNumber\":15,\"prompt\":\"a contrast between the storage characteristics of two brain structures\",\"correctAnswer\":\"B\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph B contrasts the fast-learning, temporary buffer of the hippocampus with the vast, slow-learning long-term capacity of the neocortex.\",\"evidence\":\"The hippocampus serves as a fast-learning, temporary storage buffer with rapid neuroplasticity but limited capacity. Conversely, the neocortex possesses vast long-term storage capacity but learns slowly\"},{\"id\":\"a3-q16\",\"questionNumber\":16,\"prompt\":\"the historical misconception that sleep was an inactive biological state\",\"correctAnswer\":\"A\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph A outlines the historical idea that sleep was 'a passive biological state—a temporary shutdown of consciousness during which the brain resting from the rigors of wakefulness remains largely dormant.'\",\"evidence\":\"For centuries, sleep was conceptualized as a passive biological state—a temporary shutdown of consciousness during which the brain resting from the rigors of wakefulness remains largely dormant.\"},{\"id\":\"a3-q17\",\"questionNumber\":17,\"prompt\":\"the negative cognitive consequences of missing a full night of sleep before tests\",\"correctAnswer\":\"F\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph F discusses 'all-nighter' study sessions leading to hippocampal saturation and memory decay within forty-eight hours.\",\"evidence\":\"When students pull 'all-nighter' study sessions before examinations, the hippocampal temporary buffer becomes saturated and unstable; information fails to transition to the neocortex and is rapidly forgotten within forty-eight hours.\"},{\"id\":\"a3-q18\",\"questionNumber\":18,\"prompt\":\"a description of the ultra-fast replay of neural sequences during deep sleep\",\"correctAnswer\":\"C\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph C describes sharp-wave ripples where neural firing sequences are replayed 'up to twenty times faster than real-time.'\",\"evidence\":\"During these ripples, the exact neural firing sequences that encoded waking experiences are 'replayed' in fast-forward mode, up to twenty times faster than real-time.\"},{\"id\":\"a3-q19\",\"questionNumber\":19,\"prompt\":\"the chemical role of calcium ions in strengthening dendritic synapses\",\"correctAnswer\":\"D\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph D describes how sleep spindles trigger localized influxes of calcium ions into cortical dendrites to induce long-term potentiation.\",\"evidence\":\"Crucially, sleep spindles create localized influxes of calcium ions into cortical dendrites, triggering the intracellular signaling cascades necessary for long-term potentiation\"}]},{\"groupInstruction\":\"Complete the sentences below. Choose NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS from the passage for each answer. Write your answers in boxes 20–23 on your answer sheet.\",\"questionType\":\"sentence_completion\",\"questions\":[{\"id\":\"a3-q20\",\"questionNumber\":20,\"prompt\":\"The temporary storage facility in the brain where new episodic events are initially recorded is the ____.\",\"correctAnswer\":\"hippocampus\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph B explains that new episodic experiences 'are initially encoded within the hippocampus.'\",\"evidence\":\"During wakefulness, new episodic experiences—such as memorizing a list of vocabulary words or navigating an unfamiliar city—are initially encoded within the hippocampus.\"},{\"id\":\"a3-q21\",\"questionNumber\":21,\"prompt\":\"Slow electrical oscillations that sweep across the neocortex occur primarily during deep ____.\",\"correctAnswer\":\"NREM sleep\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph C states that these oscillations take place 'during deep NREM sleep'.\",\"evidence\":\"During deep NREM sleep, the brain generates slow electrical oscillations that sweep across the neocortex at a frequency of approximately one cycle per second.\"},{\"id\":\"a3-q22\",\"questionNumber\":22,\"prompt\":\"Rhythmic bursts of brain activity produced by the thalamus are known as ____.\",\"correctAnswer\":\"sleep spindles\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph D defines sleep spindles as 'brief, rhythmic bursts of 11 to 16 Hertz brain activity generated by the thalamus'.\",\"evidence\":\"Meanwhile, sleep spindles—brief, rhythmic bursts of 11 to 16 Hertz brain activity generated by the thalamus—act as gatekeepers during this information transfer.\"},{\"id\":\"a3-q23\",\"questionNumber\":23,\"prompt\":\"During REM sleep, brain levels of the stress neurochemical ____ drop completely to zero.\",\"correctAnswer\":\"noradrenaline\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph E highlights that 'brain levels of noradrenaline—a key chemical associated with anxiety and emotional stress—drop to zero.'\",\"evidence\":\"During REM sleep, the brain's levels of noradrenaline—a key chemical associated with anxiety and emotional stress—drop to zero.\"}]},{\"groupInstruction\":\"Choose the correct letter, A, B, C, or D. Write the correct letter in boxes 24–26 on your answer sheet.\",\"questionType\":\"multiple_choice\",\"questions\":[{\"id\":\"a3-q24\",\"questionNumber\":24,\"prompt\":\"What is the primary function of the neocortex in memory formation?\",\"options\":[\"To store short-term memories at high speed\",\"To coordinate muscle movements during sleep\",\"To provide vast, slow-learning permanent storage\",\"To produce rapid noradrenaline surges\"],\"correctAnswer\":\"C\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph B states the neocortex 'possesses vast long-term storage capacity but learns slowly'.\",\"evidence\":\"Conversely, the neocortex possesses vast long-term storage capacity but learns slowly, requiring repeated, distributed reactivation\"},{\"id\":\"a3-q25\",\"questionNumber\":25,\"prompt\":\"Why is accelerated neural replay during sharp-wave ripples significant?\",\"options\":[\"It prevents the brain from waking up during loud sounds\",\"It depletes the brain's supply of calcium ions\",\"It limits the duration of dream cycles\",\"It transmits newly encoded memory traces repeatedly to the neocortex\"],\"correctAnswer\":\"D\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph C notes that accelerated replay 'repeatedly broadcasts new memory traces to the receptive neocortex, gradually etching them into long-term synaptic connections.'\",\"evidence\":\"This accelerated replay repeatedly broadcasts new memory traces to the receptive neocortex, gradually etching them into long-term synaptic connections.\"},{\"id\":\"a3-q26\",\"questionNumber\":26,\"prompt\":\"What creative advantage is facilitated by REM sleep?\",\"options\":[\"The formation of new links between disparate concepts\",\"The immediate elimination of all factual details\",\"The suppression of creative imagination\",\"The permanent shutdown of visual cortex processing\"],\"correctAnswer\":\"A\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph E explains that 'REM sleep promotes associative cross-linking, allowing the brain to forge novel connections between seemingly unrelated concepts'.\",\"evidence\":\"Furthermore, REM sleep promotes associative cross-linking, allowing the brain to forge novel connections between seemingly unrelated concepts\"}]}]},{\"passageNumber\":3,\"title\":\"Ancient Urban Water Systems: The Engineering of Qanats and Aqueducts\",\"subtitle\":\"How ancient civilizations engineered sophisticated subterranean gravity-fed hydraulics to sustain arid megacities\",\"wordCount\":880,\"text\":\"Paragraph A\\nThe survival and expansion of ancient human civilizations in arid and semi-arid zones was fundamentally governed by hydraulic engineering. Without reliable methods to capture, transport, and distribute freshwater over immense distances, the rise of major urban polities in the parched basins of Mesopotamia, the Iranian plateau, and the Mediterranean littoral would have been physically impossible. While modern societies rely heavily on fossil-fuel-powered electric turbines and pressurized piping, ancient hydraulic engineers mastered the subtle manipulation of natural gravity, subterranean geology, and thermal insulation to establish civil waterworks that operated continuously for millennia without mechanical energy.\\n\\nParagraph B\\nAmong the most sophisticated of these historic technologies was the Persian qanat system, conceived during the early first millennium BCE across what is modern-day Iran. A qanat is an gently sloping subterranean canal that taps into high-altitude mountain alluvial aquifers and guides groundwater down to arid agricultural plains and urban settlements purely through gravitational flow. The construction of a qanat began with the identification of a mother well (madar-chah) in an alluvial fan at the base of a mountain range. Once water was struck, miners known as muqannis excavated a subterranean conduit with an incline typically between 1:1,000 and 1:1,500—steep enough to prevent water from stagnating, yet gentle enough to prevent high-velocity hydraulic turbulence that would erode the unlined tunnel walls.\\n\\nParagraph C\\nTo facilitate tunneling and ensure continuous air supply for the excavators, vertical shafts were sunk from the surface at intervals of twenty to thirty meters along the conduit's path. These surface shafts also served as extraction chutes for excavated soil and provided subsequent maintenance access to remove silt. The subterranean design of the qanat offered profound ecological advantages over open-air stone canals. In arid desert climates where daytime ambient temperatures routinely exceed 45 degrees Celsius, open water canals lose up to 50 percent of their volume to solar evapotranspiration. Enclosed deep underground, qanat water remained shielded from sunlight and desert winds, arriving at urban gateways fresh, cool, and uncontaminated.\\n\\nParagraph D\\nIn contrast to the predominantly subterranean subterranean Persian qanat, the Roman Empire achieved urban water dominance through monumental elevated masonry aqueducts. The city of Rome itself was supplied by eleven independent aqueduct lines spanning over 500 kilometers in aggregate, delivering roughly one million cubic meters of fresh spring water daily. Roman hydraulic engineers favored above-ground stone bridges and arcade structures to span steep valleys, utilizing the enduring compressive strength of pozzolanic concrete—a volcanic ash composite that hardened even underwater. Within cities, incoming aqueduct channels discharged into monumental distribution reservoirs known as castella aquae. From these central towers, lead and terracotta pipes distributed water hierarchically: first to public drinking fountains accessible to all citizens, second to public thermal baths, and only surplus overflow to private luxury villas whose owners paid special municipal water tariffs.\\n\\nParagraph E\\nDespite their architectural disparities, both the Persian and Roman hydraulic traditions embodied sustainable engineering principles that offer compelling lessons for contemporary water management. Qanats, by their very design, represent an equilibrium-based extraction model. Because they depend exclusively on the natural hydraulic gradient of the water table, they cannot withdraw more water than natural mountain aquifers recharge through precipitation; during prolonged droughts, qanat outflow naturally contracts, preventing the irreversible subsidence and salinization of underground aquifers. Conversely, contemporary deep-well turbine pumping indiscriminately depletes prehistoric fossil aquifers far beyond replenishment rates. Modern urban planners are increasingly re-evaluating ancient gravity-driven, passive hydraulic architectures as resilient, zero-carbon adaptations to global climate instability.\",\"questionGroups\":[{\"groupInstruction\":\"Do the following statements agree with the views of the writer in Reading Passage 3? In boxes 27–31 on your answer sheet, write:\\nYES if the statement agrees with the views of the writer\\nNO if the statement contradicts the views of the writer\\nNOT GIVEN if it is impossible to say what the writer thinks about this\",\"questionType\":\"yes_no_not_given\",\"questions\":[{\"id\":\"a3-q27\",\"questionNumber\":27,\"prompt\":\"Ancient civil water systems depended primarily on pressurized mechanical pumps to move water across plains.\",\"correctAnswer\":\"NO\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph A states ancient engineers 'mastered the subtle manipulation of natural gravity, subterranean geology, and thermal insulation... without mechanical energy.'\",\"evidence\":\"ancient hydraulic engineers mastered the subtle manipulation of natural gravity, subterranean geology, and thermal insulation to establish civil waterworks that operated continuously for millennia without mechanical energy.\"},{\"id\":\"a3-q28\",\"questionNumber\":28,\"prompt\":\"The slope of a qanat tunnel was carefully calculated to avoid both stagnant water and wall erosion.\",\"correctAnswer\":\"YES\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph B explains the incline was 'steep enough to prevent water from stagnating, yet gentle enough to prevent high-velocity hydraulic turbulence that would erode the unlined tunnel walls.'\",\"evidence\":\"an incline typically between 1:1,000 and 1:1,500—steep enough to prevent water from stagnating, yet gentle enough to prevent high-velocity hydraulic turbulence that would erode the unlined tunnel walls.\"},{\"id\":\"a3-q29\",\"questionNumber\":29,\"prompt\":\"Surface shafts along a qanat line provided ventilation for workers during excavation.\",\"correctAnswer\":\"YES\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph C states vertical shafts were sunk 'to facilitate tunneling and ensure continuous air supply for the excavators'.\",\"evidence\":\"To facilitate tunneling and ensure continuous air supply for the excavators, vertical shafts were sunk from the surface at intervals of twenty to thirty meters along the conduit's path.\"},{\"id\":\"a3-q30\",\"questionNumber\":30,\"prompt\":\"Private Roman homeowners were granted higher priority for water distribution than public bathhouses.\",\"correctAnswer\":\"NO\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph D states water was distributed hierarchically: first to public fountains, second to public baths, and only surplus overflow to private villas.\",\"evidence\":\"From these central towers, lead and terracotta pipes distributed water hierarchically: first to public drinking fountains accessible to all citizens, second to public thermal baths, and only surplus overflow to private luxury 